<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="L-cysteine biosynthesis I" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Xref identifier="CYSTSYN-PWY" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Common Viridiplantae Pathways (CVP) generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are thought to be present in all or most Viridiplantae species including algae. All UPP pathways are automatically imported into databases regardless of Pathologic predictions. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; This pathway of cysteine biosynthesis is a two-step conversion of |FRAME: SER| to |FRAME: CYS| via |FRAME: ACETYLSERINE|. The pathway has been documented in bacteria |CITS: [Kredich96]|, archaea |CITS: [10613873]| and plants |CITS: [10940562]|. In the bacterium |FRAME: TAX-83333| both pathway steps are regulated. The first step is subject to very strong feedback inhibition by the final product, |FRAME: CYS|. The second step is regulated at the genetic level with both |FRAME: ACSERLYA-ENZRXN| isozymes being under the positive control of the cysteine-responsive |FRAME: PC00040|. |FRAME: TAX-511145| and |FRAME: TAX-90371| produce two cysteine synthase isoenzymes (|FRAME: ACSERLYA-CPLX| and |FRAME: ACSERLYB-CPLX|). |FRAME: ACSERLYB-CPLX| is preferentially used for the biosynthesis of L-cysteine during anaerobic growth conditions |CITS: [6754864]| and is able to utilize thiosulfate instead of sulfide to produce S-sulfo-L-cysteine |CITS: [6373737]|. Only one of the isoenzymes (the |FRAME: EG10192|-encoded |FRAME: ACSERLYA-CPLX|) forms a complex with |FRAME: CPLX0-237|. CysK is also the only one of the two cysteine synthases that is required for cell viability on cysteine-free medium. In higher plants |FRAME:CPLX-1323| (SAT) and |FRAME:CPLX-1326| (OAS-TL) can form a similar enzyme complex, which is thought to play an important role in regulation. Within the complex SAT activity is higher and OAS-TL activity is lower than the respective free forms. When the complex dissociates, SAT becomes inactive and OAS-TL becomes more active. The formation of the complex is regulated by the concentration of |FRAME: ACETYLSERINE| (OAS) and |FRAME: HS|, where OAS triggers the dissociation of the complex and sulfide counteracts the effect of OAS. Both pathway steps are reversible, and genetic and proteomic data suggest that the cysteine synthases may actually act as a sulfur scavenging system during sulfur starvation, stripping sulfur off of |FRAME: CYS| and generating any number of variant amino acids in the process |CITS: [Kredich96]|.</Description>
